v7: {name:'Thomas Valentine', gen:3, line:'both', conf:C.y, parent:'WV', star:true,
 role:'Book Finisher, later Foreman Bookbinder', dates:'1881 – 1937',
 summary:'My great-grandfather, and the point where the two families join. Baptised Church of Ireland like his brothers and sisters, but recorded as Roman Catholic by the time he married Anne Kavanagh at nineteen. He made foreman in the bindery, had thirteen children, and took the whole family to Scotland.',
 facts:[['Born','18 April 1881'],['Baptised','St George COI, Hardwicke Place'],
 ['Family home','31 Portland Place'],
 ['Married','5 August 1900, St Mary’s Pro-Cathedral'],['Wife','Anne Kavanagh'],
 ['Address at marriage','30 Portland Place'],
 ['1901 census','Aged 19, 152 Great Britain Street, Book Finisher, Roman Catholic'],
 ['1911 census','Aged 29, 22 Ballybough Road, Foreman Bookbinder Finisher'],
 ['1921 census','Aged 40, the Wynd, Cumbernauld, Dunbartonshire - <b>Railway Porter</b>, North British Railway Co'],
 ['Died','12 October 1937']],
 notes:['Somewhere between his Church of Ireland baptism in 1881 and his Catholic marriage in 1900 he changed denomination. My working assumption is a Catholic baptism around the age of eleven, but I haven’t confirmed it.',
 'He was there when his father died in December 1908 and gave the information for the certificate.',
 'No image survives of his civil birth registration - it may be corrupted, though the original could still be viewable in Dublin.',
 'The family emigrated to Scotland, we think in 1916 or 1917. The 1921 census finds them at the Wynd in Cumbernauld Village, fourteen people in two rooms, and Thomas is no longer a bookbinder but a <b>railway porter</b> for the North British Railway Company. Three generations in the book trade end with that entry.',
 'My reading of why is this. The value of what he knew how to do was falling away underneath him for his whole working life. Machines took over the folding, the sewing, the casing-in and the blocking, and the hand-finishing he had trained for became a shrinking niche just as he reached the top of it. He was a foreman of a trade that was disappearing. Leaving it made sense, and there would have been no point carrying it to Scotland, where the same machines were doing the same thing.',
 'We also know at least some of Anne\u2019s family had already gone to Scotland, and the family understanding is that they talked Thomas and Anne into following, with better prospects and better pay. That is the pull. The push was a dying trade.',
 'Why the railway specifically, I don\u2019t know. There is railway on both sides of the tree - his own grandfather was a railway clerk and Anne\u2019s father a railway guard - but two men a generation or two back feels like a thin explanation for a career.',
 'His two eldest sons had gone into the fireclay and brick works of the district: William with J. G. Stein &amp; Co, Thomas John with the Castlecary Fireclay Co.',
 'His children’s marriages should be found in Scottish records rather than Irish ones.'],
 sources:[['Baptism record', IG+'/view/?record_id=19d58ec1c8-10756'],
 ['St George baptism register (PDF)', IG+'/files/church/d-298-2-9-042.pdf'],
 ['Marriage 1900 - church record', IG+'/view/?record_id=76e681dcc3-5778'],
 ['Marriage 1900 - civil record (PDF)', IG+'/files/civil/marriage_returns/marriages_1900/10355/5769507.pdf'],
 ['1901 census', 'https://nationalarchives.ie/collections/search-the-census/census-record/?census_year=1901&surname__icontains=valentine&firstname__icontains=thomas&id=5180096'],
 ['1911 census', 'https://nationalarchives.ie/collections/search-the-census/census-record/?census_year=1911&surname__icontains=valentine&firstname__icontains=william&id=48711'],
 ['1921 census of Scotland - Cumbernauld 495/9/19', SP]]},

