const QUESTIONS = [
 {p:1, q:'Where and when was William Valentine born?', d:'The oldest thing I can’t answer. No birth or baptism, because civil registration didn’t start until 1864. The closest candidate, a William Valentine born in Dublin in 1840 to John and Charlotte, I looked at and rejected. His parents were William, a railway clerk, and Elizabeth, of 93 Marlboro Street.',
 n:'The 1841 and 1851 census search forms, made for Old Age Pension claims after 1908, are the standard way round this. William could never have claimed himself. He was 67 when he died that December, three years short of 70, and the scheme started the following month. But a sibling or a cousin who did claim would have named the same parents and the same address in 1841 or 1851.'},
 {p:1, q:'Why was Martha baptised as Martha Herbert?', d:'The 1846 baptism has the right year and the right sponsors, Patrick and Catherine Mackey, who are the names she gave as her parents at her wedding. The surname is wrong. And no Martha Mackey was born anywhere in Ireland in the surrounding years, which makes me more inclined to believe it, not less.',
 n:'Worth testing whether Herbert was a first husband of Catherine’s, a stepfather, or an illegitimacy later tidied up. The full register page either side of the entry might show a pattern.'},
 {p:1, q:'Who was James Kavanagh’s first wife, and are there half-siblings?', d:'He is a widower at his 1873 marriage, aged about 26. So there was a marriage in the 1860s or early 1870s and possibly children. Nobody has looked for any of them.',
 n:'A whole branch nobody has touched. I’d start with Kavanagh and Cavanagh marriages in north Dublin between 1866 and 1872 with a John Kavanagh, coachman, as father.'},
 {p:1, q:'Where did James and Anne Kavanagh go after 1901?', d:'Neither is in the 1911 census. No James Kavanagh of the right age is married to an Anne. The only one close on age is 60 and married to a Sarah. No Anne Kavanagh in the range is a widow, and no Anne Walsh fits either.',
 n:'Anne was certainly still alive. She was at her grandson’s birth in November 1911 at 177 Great Britain Street, and at 22 Kings Avenue in 1914. Working back from those two addresses through the 1911 street returns might find her under a mangled name.<br><br>There is a better lead now. We know at least some of Anne’s family had already moved to Scotland before Thomas and Anne followed, which makes Scotland the obvious place to look for her brothers James and John - and possibly for the parents too. I have been searching the wrong country.'},
 {p:2, q:'What became of Hannah Valentine?', d:'22 in 1901, unmarried, no occupation. No marriage, no death, nothing after that at all.', n:'Emigration is the obvious explanation. Passenger lists and Scottish or English records are worth a look, given where the rest of them ended up.'},
 {p:2, q:'What became of Reuben John Valentine?', d:'A scholar of 13 in 1901. No marriage, no death, and I tried John Valentine as well as Reuben John.', n:'Same as Hannah. Emigration, and the possibility he used only one of his two forenames as an adult.'},
 {p:2, q:'Is the 1921 Martha Byrne death the right one?', d:'The age fits exactly, 50, born 1869, and asthenia and house maid are both plausible. But it says spinster and she was a widow by 1901.', n:'The 1911 entry for a Martha Mary Byrne, 40, widow, and Martha Lillian Byrne, 13, boarding at 13 Wood Lane may be her and her daughter. Confirm that and the 1921 record can be tested against it.'},
 {p:2, q:'Is the 1910 Mary Valentine death the right one?', d:'I wrote “not convinced” against it at the time. The occupation is unclear and I never settled whether Isabella was a confirmation name.', n:'Check whether any other Mary Valentine of about 19 died in Dublin around then, and look for a burial record that names the parents.'},
 {p:2, q:'Where is the death of James Valentine, born 1902?', d:'The 1911 arithmetic, seven born and six living, says he died before April 1911. I searched infant Valentine deaths in Dublin from 1900 to 1911 and found nothing that fits.',
 n:'Either it was registered under a variant surname, or they had already made a first move out of Dublin. Both worth testing.'},
 {p:1, q:'Where are Brendon’s and Mary’s birth registrations?', d:'The 1921 census gives 2 years 3 months and 8 months, so around March 1919 and October 1920 at Cumbernauld. I have not seen either certificate, so both dates are calculated rather than documented. Brendon’s would pin down when they actually arrived.',
 n:'Scottish statutory birth registers for Cumbernauld, district 495, 1919 and 1920. They will also give Thomas’s occupation at each date, which should show whether he was already on the railway when he got there.'},
 {p:2, q:'What took them to Cumbernauld, and when exactly?', d:'The window is now narrow, between November 1915 and 1919. That covers the Rising, conscription-era Britain and the start of the War of Independence. Cumbernauld is an odd choice. A village rather than a city, with brickworks and a railway and no book trade at all.',
 n:'The Castlecary and Bonnybridge fireclay works pulled labour from Ireland and the North British Railway ran through Castlecary. That combination is probably the answer. Valuation rolls for Cumbernauld and any surviving railway staff registers would date his employment.'},
 {p:2, q:'Why did Thomas give up the book trade?', d:'Foreman bookbinder finisher in Dublin in 1911, railway porter in Cumbernauld in 1921. On the face of it that is a long way down in skill and status, and it ends three generations in the trade.<br><br>My reading is that it only looks like a fall if you assume the trade was still worth something. It wasn’t. Mechanisation had been eating the craft for forty years, and hand finishing was a shrinking niche by the time he was running a bindery floor. He was at the top of something that was going away, and there was no reason to carry it to Scotland, where the same machines were doing the same work. The trade he left was already dying.',
 n:'Worth finding out whether the bindery he worked for closed, and whether there was any finishing work to be had in Scotland at all. His 1937 death certificate will give a final occupation.<br><br>The open part is the railway. There is railway on both sides of the tree - William Valentine senior was a railway clerk and James Kavanagh a railway guard - but that is two men a generation or more back, and it feels tenuous to me. Employment records for the North British Railway, if any survive for Castlecary, would be the place to test it.'},
 {p:2, q:'Where did Thomas and Anne die?', d:'My chart gives 12 October 1937 and 18 June 1958. Neither has a source attached and I have never checked either against a certificate. On the evidence both are far more likely Scottish than Irish.',
 n:'Scottish statutory death registers. They will confirm the dates, give the addresses, and for Anne in 1958 name an informant who is probably one of my grandparents’ generation.'},
 {p:3, q:'Were Mary Ellen and Christina McCarthy sisters?', d:'Robert married Mary Ellen McCarthy in 1894 and Louis married Christina McCarthy in 1900. I flagged “2 sisters?” against both at the time and never followed it up.', n:'Both certificates name the fathers. If they match, that settles it in one check.'},
 {p:3, q:'Was Thomas baptised a second time as a Roman Catholic?', d:'Baptised at St George’s in April 1881, married in the Pro-Cathedral as a Catholic in 1900, recorded as Roman Catholic in the 1901 census. My assumption is a Catholic baptism around the age of eleven.', n:'Pro-Cathedral baptism registers for roughly 1892 would confirm it.'},
 {p:3, q:'Who were Robert Valintine and Mary Markey?', d:'They sponsored Martha’s 1869 baptism. The spellings are variants of Valentine and Mackey, so they are relatives on both sides. I just don’t know which.', n:'A brother of William and a sister of Martha are the obvious guesses. Either would be the first lateral relatives I have in that generation.'},
 {p:3, q:'Which Elizabeth Byrne is ours?', d:'Elizabeth Valentine married Matthew J Byrne in 1903. Around 794 Elizabeth Byrnes died in Ireland between 1903 and 1954.', n:'Narrowing by parish or address, or just finding the couple in the 1911 census, would cut that right down.'}
];
